  <title>Getting Started</title>

  <p>
    Babble is a Mastodon client. It reads and posts on any Mastodon
    server, and on the other servers that server federates with.
  </p>

  <section id="server">
    <title>Your server</title>
    <p>
      Type your server's address on the welcome screen and choose
      <gui>Continue</gui> or <gui>Continue with…</gui>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Your browser opens the server's own sign-in page. Babble never sees
      your password: the server hands back a token, and only that token is
      stored, in your system keyring.
    </p>
    <note style="important">
      <title>Ask only once</title>
      <p>
        Babble asks for the <sys>read write</sys> scopes. Moderation
        permissions are requested separately, and only when you open the
        Admin Dashboard.
      </p>
    </note>
    <p>
      If the browser does not come back on its own, Babble offers an
      <gui>Enter Authorization Code…</gui> button so you can paste the code
      the server shows you by hand.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="accounts">
    <title>Accounts</title>
    <p>
      Babble keeps every login in the system keyring, so it needs a keyring
      service such as GNOME Keyring or KeePassXC to be running. If none is,
      Babble says so when it starts or when you sign in, and keeps the
      account it already has. Start a keyring service and restart Babble.
    </p>
    <p>
      Once you are in, <link xref="reading">Reading</link> covers the
      feeds, and <link xref="writing">Writing</link> covers the composer.
    </p>
  </section>

</page>